# Zeros — files to seed into every new worktree
# ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# `git worktree add` gives you TRACKED files only. A fresh workspace therefore
# starts with no .env, no project MCP config, and none of the gitignored
# machine-local state the project needs to build, run, or read its own working
# notes. This file lists the paths Zeros copies out of the MAIN checkout into
# each new worktree at creation time.
#
# Implementation: apps/desktop/src/engine/git/files-to-copy.ts.
#
# Three things about the syntax are worth knowing before you edit this:
#
#   1. ANCHOR YOUR PATTERNS. A pattern with no leading `/` is matched by
#      BASENAME AT ANY DEPTH. A bare `.env` matches any nested app's `.env` and
#      copies that app's secrets into every workspace; a bare `docs/` expands to
#      `**/docs/**`, which is 159 paths in this tree — 95 of them inside
#      node_modules (measured, not guessed). Anchor with `/`, and only step
#      outside that rule deliberately (there is exactly one exception below,
#      and it is called out).
#   2. It is plain gitignore syntax, matched by git itself (`git ls-files -o -i
#      --exclude-from`), so `!` negation works and behaves exactly as it does in
#      .gitignore: LAST MATCH WINS. `!x` before a broad pattern that re-covers
#      `x` does NOT exclude it. Narrowing the positive pattern is still clearer
#      than subtracting from a broad one.
#   3. This file REPLACES the built-in default (`.env*`); it is not merged with
#      it. Anything the default used to cover has to be spelled out here, which
#      is why `/.env*` leads the list. An EMPTY file therefore means "copy
#      nothing" — delete the file instead if you want the default back.
#
# Copies are read from the MAIN checkout's working tree, so the canonical .env
# has to live at the repo root of the main clone. A .env created inside a
# worktree is local to that worktree and is never propagated anywhere.
#
# MIND THE MATCH SET SIZE — and not for local reasons. A CLOUD workspace has no
# local checkout to copy from, so the whole match set is uploaded inline in the
# create request (POST /workspaces), which is size-capped server-side. Blow past
# that cap and EVERY CLOUD CREATE FAILS with `413 FST_ERR_CTP_BODY_TOO_LARGE`,
# surfaced in the UI as the unrelated-looking "Cloud workspace isn't reachable
# right now (it may be starting)" — a symptom that points nowhere near this file.
#
# `/docs/` is the cautionary precedent: 64 files / 2.27 MiB, which took the
# request to ~2.3x the cap (measured 2026-07-27, not estimated). Trimming could
# not rescue it — `/docs/` and `/docs/*` expand to the same 64 files through
# toPathspecs(), and dropping even the largest subtree still left 0.98 MiB. What
# fixed it was TRACKING docs/ instead (2026-07-28), which is the right answer for
# bulk content generally: `git worktree add` materialises tracked files for free
# and they never enter the create request at all. The list below is back to
# 4 files / ~3 KB. Keep it there.
#
# This file is safe to commit: it lists patterns, never values. Everything it
# names is gitignored, so a seeded copy cannot be accidentally committed from
# the worktree either.
#
# Deliberately NOT seeded (each verified, not assumed):
#   styles/Artifacts/           the design lab is TRACKED, so `git worktree add`
#                               already materialises it. Seeding a tracked path
#                               would copy the main checkout's working-tree
#                               version over the branch's own.
#   docs/                       same reason — TRACKED as of 2026-07-28. It used
#                               to be seeded from here, and that is exactly what
#                               pushed the create request past the cap (above).
#   node_modules/               installed by the workspace setup script; copying
#                               a dependency tree is slower and less correct
#                               than running the install.
#   .codex-protocol-cache/      multi-GB Rust workspace, only touched when
#                               package.json#codexProtocolVersion is bumped. The
#                               generated bindings AND their `.version` stamp
#                               are committed, so codegen short-circuits in a
#                               fresh checkout (scripts/codegen-codex.mjs).
#   snapshots/current|diff/     visual-harness OUTPUT, rewritten every run; the
#                               baselines it compares against are tracked.
#   testing/                    a dead ignore rule — nothing in the tree reads
#                               or writes that path, so there is nothing to seed.
#   apps/{web,marketing}/.env*  neither Cloudflare app reads a local env file;
#                               deployments receive configuration from
#                               Cloudflare. Add an explicit pattern only if that
#                               boundary changes.
#   .zeros/settings.local.toml  the repo-local settings layer already resolves
#                               from the main checkout for a linked worktree;
#                               the workspace-local layer is meant to start
#                               empty (apps/desktop/src/engine/settings/ops.ts).

# Machine-local environment. `/.env*` is the anchored form of the built-in
# default: the root `.env` plus `.env.local` and friends — and nothing from a
# sibling directory that happens to keep an env file of its own.
/.env*
/apps/control-plane/.env*

# Cloudflare Workers local secrets, read by `wrangler dev` from the worker's own
# directory. THE ONE UNANCHORED PATTERN: which package owns a worker changes
# over time, and this matches nothing else in the tree (verified: 0 matches
# today, including inside node_modules).
**/.dev.vars

# Project-scoped MCP servers. Gitignored, but apps/desktop/src/engine/agents/mcp-scan.ts
# reads `<repo>/.mcp.json` per repo root — so without this a new workspace
# silently loses every project-level MCP server.
/.mcp.json

# External worktree-tool config. Only `.conductor/settings.local.toml` is
# gitignored now — `settings.toml` is TRACKED (see .gitignore), so it arrives via
# `git worktree add` like any other tracked file and correctly falls OUT of this
# match set. The directory pattern is kept rather than narrowed so any future
# machine-local file under .conductor/ is seeded without another edit here.
# Seeded so the per-workspace setup command survives into new worktrees instead
# of silently reverting to the tool's defaults.
/.conductor/
